      "writtenOn": "2026-02-27T00:00:10Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "nit: in the ideal world, UV444 would use the ARGBToY.  But Y is unsigned and UV is signed, and with 8 bit it helps to use different instructions/datatype.\n\nmany YUV formats have the U and V together (biplanar), but with planar something like ARGBToU could do 1 plane and get called twice.\nARM and Intel prefer multiple reads but 1 write per loop, performance wise.\nthe u and v could be weaved.\n\nARGBToUV does subsampling.  I had hoped we could do ARGBToUV444 and then downsample, or downsample the ARGB first into a row buffer.  But as is, ARGBToUV helps performance a bit.   It is complicated and limits the performance on intel compared to ARGBToY.  I dont have avx512 for the UV versions yet.",
      "revId": "7448e671d509e84ecefe2bfd62de949e30ad060b",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
